    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-115593",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1b0dd714-5bf1-573f-9ff9-11802622ce92",
      "knowledge_point": "Estimating the Value of a Function at a Point Given its Derivative",
      "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ and $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to estimate the value of $f(1).$",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ then ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
