      "knowledge_point_id": "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate",
      "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(1,2)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ estimate the value of $f(0.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(1,2),$ so we have $f(1)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(-2)(x-1) \\\\  & =2-2(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.8) & ≈L(0.8) \\\\  & =2-2(0.8-1) \\\\  & =2-2(-0.2) \\\\  & =2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(0,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph close to $x=1,$ and therefore we have an underestimate.",
